# world-6dof.xrs - does the world hold still and stay square under 6DOF head
# movement, or does it warp/swim?
#
# Warping is normally a headset-only complaint because a human cannot repeat a
# head motion precisely. Here the motion is exact and repeatable, so two runs are
# comparable and a regression is visible as a number.
#
# What each capture is for:
#   yaw_-30 .. yaw_+30   pure rotation. The world should rotate rigidly. A
#                        claimed-fov error shows up as the scene appearing to
#                        magnify or shear as you turn - check derived.claimRatioH
#                        stays ~1.0 at EVERY yaw, not just at zero.
#   pitch_up/down        the vertical axis, where an aspect or fov-v mistake hides.
#   trans_*              pure translation. Near geometry must shift MORE than far
#                        geometry (parallax). If everything shifts by the same
#                        amount the head translation is being applied to the whole
#                        projection instead of the camera.
#
# Numeric checks that need no eyes:
#   $shots | ForEach-Object { $_.ClaimRatioH }        # all ~1.0
#   $shots | ForEach-Object { $_.EyeSeparationM }     # all == ipd, stereo intact
#
# Visual check, per capture: img-diff left vs right. Parallax must concentrate on
# NEAR geometry - that is what correct stereo looks like.
reset
@mod vrstereo on
@frames 60
head rot 0 0 0
@frames 15
@shot w_yaw_00
head rot -30 0 0
@frames 15
@shot w_yaw_n30
head rot 30 0 0
@frames 15
@shot w_yaw_p30
head rot 0 20 0
@frames 15
@shot w_pitch_up
head rot 0 -20 0
@frames 15
@shot w_pitch_dn
head rot 0 0 0
@frames 15
head pos 0.4 1.6 0
@frames 15
@shot w_trans_x
head pos 0 1.6 -0.4
@frames 15
@shot w_trans_z
head pos 0 1.25 0
@frames 15
@shot w_crouch
head pose 0 1.6 0 0 0 0
@frames 15
